# Volume of the simulation box
vol: VOLUME
# Total potential energy of the simulation box
G: ENERGY

# PLUMED PBC is somewhat initially uintuitive. It will treat the biundaries as the 0 coordinate from both sides, and thus always try to pick the one closest to 0
# We will use NOPBC for position, so that it doesn't do this and does cartesian coordinates normally. We do need to keep it enabled for COM however
c: COM ATOMS=42774-42785
p: POSITION ATOM=c NOPBC

# Our function for biasing the simulation
# Based on https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.jcim.9b00834 we will pull our molecule with force F(z) = k(delta_z - vt)
# Where k = 600kJ/(mol nm^2), v = 0.5nm/ns to 5nm/ns
# Although we will use v=0.2nm/ns to run for longer (40ns to cross our 8nm box)
# Note that the nature paper: https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-019-42272-0 does not do this. They just crank the temperature up and do unbiased simulations instead
# Based on the PLUMED docs, this should replicate what happens in our first paper
# Also note that our bias value here is a potential not a force, and we can derive it using the fact F=-du/dz
# Finally, note that t is in ps not ns

# In terms of how we run this thing, this bit of code essentially creates a moving wall that bounces our ligand back and forth from 8.5 to 0.5 in p ps 
t: TIME

MOVINGRESTRAINT ...
  ARG=p.z
  STEP0=0        AT0=8.0 KAPPA0=500.0
  STEP1=50000000 AT1=1.0 KAPPA1=500.0
  LABEL=r
... MOVINGRESTRAINT


# Since we're running a biased simulation, we need to reqeight the bias when computing our free energy
rwbias: REWEIGHT_BIAS ARG=r.bias # TEMP=310
h: HISTOGRAM ARG=p.z STRIDE=1 GRID_MIN=0.5 GRID_MAX=8.5 GRID_BIN=800 BANDWIDTH=0.1 NORMALIZATION=true LOGWEIGHTS=rwbias

# Convert the histogram to free energy
fe: CONVERT_TO_FES GRID=h # TEMP=310

# This should leave a little bit of space for our molecule to wander around, but not enough to affect the membrane

DUMPGRID GRID=fe FILE=smd_oneshot_fe STRIDE=2500000
PRINT ARG=G,vol,p.x,p.y,p.z,r.bias,r.work,rwbias STRIDE=1000 FILE=smd_oneshot_out
